  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/myloplus-torquatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Myloplus torquatus grows to approx. 25 cm. It has been known scientifically since 1858 and was at times assigned to the genera Myletes and Myleus. A very good recognition feature is the black fringe in the caudal fin, a colour feature not known in any other silver dollar species. Small juveniles look completely different.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/myloplus-torquatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Myloplus torquatus is widely distributed in tropical South America and is found in the basins of both the Amazon and Orinoco rivers. The fish described here came from Venezuela.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/myloplus-torquatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Animals about 7-10 cm long are already beginning to differentiate sexually. The males develop a more extensive anal fin, and their dorsal fin takes on a broader, blunted shape compared to that of the females.</p>"
    }
  ],
